DETAILED EXPLANATION — Concept 2: Read a File Line by Line

This program shows how to open an existing text file and read its
content back, one line at a time, using std::ifstream.

- Header files: <fstream> supplies ifstream, the input file stream
  class. <string> is needed because std::getline() reads data into
  a std::string object, not a C-style char array.

- Opening the file: std::ifstream inputFile("message.txt"); opens
  the file in read mode by default (std::ios::in). Unlike ofstream,
  opening in read mode does not create or erase the file — if the
  file does not exist, the stream simply fails to open.

- Error checking: if (!inputFile) again uses the overloaded bool
  conversion on the stream object. This is essential here because
  attempting to read from a file that never opened would silently
  produce no output rather than crashing, making bugs hard to spot
  without this check.

- Reading line by line: std::getline(inputFile, line) reads
  characters from the stream into the string "line" until it hits
  a newline character (which it discards) or reaches end-of-file.
  It returns the stream itself, which is implicitly converted to
  bool in the while condition — true while a line was successfully
  read, false once end-of-file is reached or an error occurs.

- Why not use eof() in the loop condition: A common beginner
  mistake is writing "while (!inputFile.eof())". This is unsafe
  because eof() only becomes true AFTER a read attempt has failed
  past the end of the file, which can cause the last line to be
  processed twice or an extra empty iteration to run. Using the
  return value of getline() directly avoids this problem entirely.

- Printing content: Each retrieved line is printed with '\n' added
  back, since getline() strips the original newline from the file.

- Closing the file: inputFile.close() releases the file handle.
  For read-only operations this is less critical than for writes
  (no data loss risk), but it remains good practice, especially if
  the same file will be reopened later in the program (as happens
  in Concept 4, the file-copy program).

Why this matters: This getline()-based loop is the standard,
safe pattern for reading text files line by line in C++, and it
is reused directly inside later programs such as file copying,
line/word counting, and student record processing.
